---
description: Prevent pixels, props, and effects from crossing sprite-frame boundaries
alwaysApply: true
---

# Sprite-sheet frame isolation

Apply this whenever creating, editing, extracting, packing, reviewing, or
verifying a sprite sheet.

- Treat declared frame cells as authoritative. The character, clothing, held
  objects, detached props, particles, shadows, and projectiles belonging to a
  frame must remain wholly inside that frame's cell.
- Leave a fully transparent safety gutter between opaque pixels and every cell
  edge shared with another frame. No opaque or partially transparent pixel may
  touch or cross a shared boundary.
- Never infer pose ownership from list position, visual order, or a crop that
  cuts through opaque pixels. Assign source components to an exact named frame
  and keep detached components with that frame.
- If poses overlap in source art, do not conceal the problem with tighter
  cropping. Re-extract from genuinely isolated source poses or rerender only
  the affected artwork while preserving identity, anatomy, contacts, scale,
  timing, and accepted dimensions.
- Inspect the final production strip frame by frame. Source files or successful
  generation alone are not proof of completion.
- Add or extend a deterministic verifier so every new or changed sheet fails
  validation when a shared frame edge contains alpha or source pixels can be
  assigned to more than one frame.

